 ],
 "answer_text": "Found 5 pubmed records: On tree longevity. [6], Tree mycorrhizal type and tree diversity shape the forest soil microbiota. [7], Gradient Boosted Tree Approaches for Mapping European Organization for Research and Treatment of Cancer Quality of Life Questionnaire Core 30 Onto 5-Level Version of EQ-5D Index for Patients With Cancer. [8], Tree diversity effects on productivity depend on mycorrhizae and life strategies in a temperate forest experiment. [9] and Seasonal patterns of nonstructural carbohydrate storage and mobilization in two tree species with distinct life-history traits. [10]. \n\nTree Longevity\n\nPublished evidence indicates that trees do not die from genetically programmed meristem senescence but are killed by external agents or disturbance events, and that sustained growth or growth-rate plasticity is required to achieve maximum lifespan [1]. The authors also challenge the classic paradigm that shade-tolerant, late-successional species invariably live longer, noting that extremely long-lived species do not fit that model [1]. \n\nMycorrhizal Type and Soil Microbiota\n\nTree mycorrhizal type significantly affects fungal but not bacterial alpha diversity, and higher tree diversity promotes convergence of soil microbial communities [2]. In a separate diversity experiment, species richness strengthened positive productivity relationships over time, with AM and EM trees showing opposite productivity trajectories, though mixing the two mycorrhizal types produced no overyielding in young stands [3]. \n\nNonstructural Carbohydrate Dynamics\n\nSeasonal NSC pools peak in autumn in a deciduous broadleaf species and in summer in an evergreen conifer, and larger storage is not necessarily associated with more pronounced seasonal fluctuation [4]. \n\nOne retrieved study used gradient boosted tree machine learning to map an oncology quality-of-life questionnaire onto a health utility index, finding no improvement in RMSE or MAE over regression approaches [5]. \n\nPubmed records found\n\nPubmed title: On tree longevity [6]. Pubmed title: Tree mycorrhizal type and tree diversity shape the forest soil microbiota [7]. Pubmed title: Gradient Boosted Tree Approaches for Mapping European Organization for Research and Treatment of Cancer Quality of Life Questionnaire Core 30 Onto 5-Level Version of EQ-5D Index for Patients With Cancer [8]. Pubmed title: Tree diversity effects on productivity depend on mycorrhizae and life strategies in a temperate forest experiment [9]. Pubmed title: Seasonal patterns of nonstructural carbohydrate storage and mobilization in two tree species with distinct life-history traits [10]. "
